s I assume that you won't be  backing the Lib Dem an SNP call to try  and get an election by December the 9th  either it's the same the same point  you've got to solve the brexit problem  and then  of course we will have to have a general  election don't you course and when we do  have an election are you still hoping to  stand as a conservative candidate well  at the moment that's not possible  because I don't have the conservative  whip and I am NOT going to change what I  say and what I believe and what I think  about what is important for Britain's  future in any kind of attempt to sort of  sneak back in and curry favor with the  conservative leadership I hope that the  party leadership would recognize in due  course that abandoning the concept of a  broadchurch conservative party that  embraces people that have a wide range  of views abandoning that would be  electorally very damaging but I'm not  holding my breath  would you run as an independent then if  you aren't offered a way back into the  party that's my intention yes okay now  let's talk about the bill because it's  pretty clear that you want to get that  withdrawal agreement bill back on the  table really scrutinize it but would you  vote for it and continue to vote for it  as it currently stands no I think that I  voted for it at second reading  we now need a sensible timetable motion  so we have let's say four days to  scrutinize it in the Commons in  committee by the way if the government  had pressed on with this last week we  could virtually have completed that  process by now I I want to see  parliamentary oversight of the next  stage of negotiation strengthened  because let's be very clear the  so-called deal that Boris has presented  represents a final solution only for 2%  of the UK's population the 2% that live  in Northern Ireland for the other 98%  will be left dangling not knowing  whether we're going to end up with a  real deal with the European Union or  whether we're going to effectively be in  a no deal situation at the end of 2020  that's just more uncertainty for  business and for people as they go about  their day-to-day affairs so what we need  to do is strengthen parliamentary  control of the process now that's not a  guarantee of anything of course the next  Parliament may have a clear majority for  a hard brexit in which case that's what  it  will pursue that's how democracy works  but we can at least ensure that  Parliament not the government has  control of that process I mean there's  likely to be several amendments to the  bill and not least one for a second  referendum I remember and the last time  that I interviewed you and what you said  yeah a second reference it's a credible  action yes you said it's a perfectly  coherent position many people will be  strongly opposed to it but it is a  coherent position it deserves to be  considered so would you back an  amendment for a second referendum  probably not so saying that it's a  coherent proposition it deserves to be  considered is not the same as saying I  would back it I have long said that the  only way we're going to solve the crisis  in our society and our politics that  brexit has created is by a compromise a  deal which represents a bit of  give-and-take between the leave and the  remain sides of the argument a  referendum just asks the question again  between two binary outcomes and even if  the numbers move will leave a very large  proportion of the population feeling  that they've lost out and don't have a  stake in Britain's future so I don't  think repeating the question is the  right way forward I think a compromise  deal is the right way forward and could  that include a customs union for example  that's another amendment that could very  well be put would that be the kind of  compromise that you could support yes I  could support a customs union customs  union would overcome the problem that  this the there are two problems at the  center of this deal the first one is  