 mean you of course have had  conversations with many people you know  in the in Brussels what's your sense  about how long that extension could be  what we mandated the Prime Minister to  ask for a three-month extension to the  31st of January and we did that on the  basis of our best assessment of what was  the right balance between views at home  and views in Europe about what an  extension could look like  I think 31st of January is the is the  right answer and probably the answer  that the European Union will give but  I'm sure they will make it very clear  that if we get a deal done and ratified  before then even if we get a deal done  in November then that extension can be  brought to an end very quickly possibly  within days so that you know an end of  November exit is a perfectly feasible  proposition if the government just stops  messing about tables a sensible  timetable tomorrow for consideration of  the bill  timetable that would give us perhaps a  week or so in the Commons and then the  House of Lords will need a couple of  weeks and and then we can get this done  you say the government's messing about  but I just wonder if actually Boris  Johnson deserves a little bit more  credit than some people are giving him I  mean many people said he would never  going to be able to negotiate a brexit  deal in the time and yet he has he's  come back with a deal he's got it closer  than Teresa may ever did to getting  majority in the House of Commons surely  it's now time for you to step back and  say fair enough I underestimated you  well I've never underestimated Boris his  skill as a political operator and I've  said that consistently that he may be  able to do things that his predecessor  was unable to do because he's a very  slick political operator but the deal  that Boris has done is a deal that was  available to Theresa May 15 months ago a  deal that would have split Northern  Ireland from the rest of the UK Theresa  May rejected that Boris agreed with her  at the time that that was not something  any British Prime Minister could agree  he said as recently as September the  11th that a Northern Ireland only  backstop would be completely  unacceptable to the UK so pivoting to  accept something that's previously been  on offer from the EU it was their  preferred solution and has been rejected  because it didn't work for the UK is an  achievement maybe but a limited  achievement I think so he's put that on  the table now and people will want to  look very carefully at the detail of how  it works but Parliament has clearly  signaled that it is willing to get to  work on this bill so does this deal  damage the Union potentially it does I  say potentially because if the UK has a  very close relationship with the EU at  quote a free tariff free-trade alignment  of regulations for example then that  means that trade between Great Britain  and Northern Ireland will flow freely  and without any interruptions and any  duplications but if we have a different  relationship a WTO relationship with the  EU then there will be a hard border  between Great Britain and Northern  Ireland and that would be extremely  damaging to the Union  just finally could have in the past I  think people saw you as a bit of a  classic conservative yeah you're a  skeptic Chancellor former Foreign  Secretary and now you have morphed into  a rebel so I just trying to work out how  are you finding life now is it kind of  troublesome backbencher well I haven't  changed my position what's happened is  that the government that that I  desperately want to support has changed  its view and is now presenting a deal  which could head to a No Deal WTO  outcome at the end of 2020 and that's  something I am very clear we must avoid  for the sake of our economy and for the  sake of the United Kingdom so I will do  what I have to do and say what I have to  say it's not a comfortable position I'm  not a natural rebel I want to be a part  of the conservative family a 